  ],
  "text": "Release every bit of carbon dioxide locked up on Mars and you still fall far short of breathable air. The idea sounds like a construction project. It is a supply problem first. Mars has about one percent of Earth's surface pressure. You would have to multiply it enormously. The accessible carbon dioxide, in the ice caps and the soil, is nowhere near enough. And even if it were, the planet would not hold on to it. Mars has no global magnetic field, so the solar wind strips the upper atmosphere away. And its gravity is about a third of ours, which does not help either. Then the ground itself. The soil is full of perchlorates, which are toxic to people. So domes and buried habitats are plausible this century. Terraforming a planet is not. It is not a hard engineering problem. It is a physics problem with several unsolved parts. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
